0 -> [Why wouldn't you be able to detect the mites when closely inspecting rust mite on grapevine, a symptom frequently mistaken for downy mildew?]
The image you've provided shows a close-up of a grapevine leaf with symptoms that are indeed often confused with downy mildew, a common fungal disease that affects grapevines. Downy mildew is characterized by a powdery, white to grayish-white, velvety growth on the underside of the leaves, which can sometimes be mistaken for the presence of mites.

However, rust mites, which are tiny arachnids, are not visible to the naked eye when inspecting a leaf for symptoms of downy mildew. Rust mites are so small that they require a magnifying glass or a microscope to be seen. They are typically not the primary symptom of downy mildew, but rather a secondary symptom that can occur in conjunction with the disease.

The symptoms of downy mildew include the powdery growth on the underside of the leaves, as well as yellowing or browning of the leaves, and sometimes a yellow or white powdery substance on the upper surface of the leaves. Rust mites, on the other hand, can cause symptoms such as tiny, round, yellowish or brownish spots on the leaves, which can be mistaken for rust, but are actually the mites feeding on the leaf tissue.

In summary, the mites themselves are not visible to the naked eye when inspecting for downy mildew, but their presence can be inferred from the symptoms they cause, such as the tiny, round, yellowish or

