0 -> [Potato plants located at RHS Wisley]
The image shows potato plants with some signs of disease or damage. The leaves have brown spots and discoloration, which could be indicative of a fungal infection such as early blight (caused by the fungus Alternaria solani) or septoria leaf spot (caused by the fungus Septoria tubacearum). These diseases are common in potato crops and can lead to significant yield loss if not managed properly.

Symptoms of early blight include dark brown to black spots that can expand and merge, often with a yellow halo around them. Septoria leaf spot typically causes smaller, more numerous spots that are often irregular in shape and may have a yellow or white center.

To manage these diseases, it's important to practice good cultural controls such as ensuring good air circulation around the plants, avoiding overhead irrigation to reduce moisture, and removing infected leaves to prevent the spread of the disease. Chemical controls may also be necessary, involving the use of fungicides that are appropriate for the specific disease present. It's always best to consult with a local extension service or a plant pathologist for specific advice on managing potato diseases in your area.

