0 -> [What distinguishes these nitrogen-fixing nodules from nematode galls caused by Meloidogyne species to indicate that they are not the latter?]
The image shows a cluster of white, bean-like structures on the roots of a plant, which are characteristic of nitrogen-fixing nodules. These nodules are formed by legume plants in response to symbiotic bacteria, typically of the genus Rhizobium, that live within the nodules and convert atmospheric nitrogen into a form the plant can use. The texture and shape of these nodules are quite different from the galls caused by Meloidogyne nematodes. Nematode galls are typically round or oval, have a smooth surface, and are often found on the roots or stems of plants. They are usually smaller and more uniform in size compared to the irregular, elongated shape of the nitrogen-fixing nodules. Additionally, the nodules are often associated with the presence of legume plants, which can be inferred from the plant material in the background of the image.

