0 -> [An ein4 mutant never binds ethylene.  In the presence of ethylene what phenotype will be observed for the seedling triple response in this ein4 mutant?]
The image you've provided appears to be a diagram of the ethylene signaling pathway in plants, showing the interaction of ethylene with various proteins and the subsequent activation of downstream responses. The diagram includes the ethylene receptor ETR1, the ethylene response proteins EIN4, EIN3, and CTR1, and the transcription factors EIN2 and EIN3.

In the presence of ethylene, the ethylene receptor ETR1 binds ethylene, leading to the activation of EIN4. EIN4 then activates EIN3, which in turn activates CTR1. CTR1 inhibits the activity of EIN2, which normally activates the transcription of ethylene-responsive genes. The absence of EIN4, as indicated by the "ein4 mutant," would mean that ethylene cannot activate EIN3, and consequently, CTR1 will not be inhibited. This would lead to the activation of EIN2 and the transcription of ethylene-responsive genes, resulting in the seedling triple response phenotype.

The seedling triple response phenotype includes:

1. Curled leaves (Cu+)
2. Reduced root growth (Cu2+)
3. Increased ethylene production (Cu3+)

In the ein4 mutant, since ethylene cannot activate EIN3, the CTR1 will not be inhibited, and EIN2 will remain active. This means that ethylene-responsive genes will be transcribed, leading to the observed phenotype of increased ethylene

