0 -> [Interveinal chlorosis," a symptom observed on these cherry leaves where the areas between the leaf veins have turned yellow, is typically indicative of a lack of what nutrient?]
Interveinal chlorosis, as seen on the cherry leaves in the image, is often indicative of a deficiency in nitrogen (N) or sometimes in iron (Fe). However, the yellowing between the veins is more commonly associated with a nitrogen deficiency. This occurs when the plant is not receiving enough nitrogen, which is essential for the production of chlorophyll, the green pigment in leaves that is crucial for photosynthesis. Without sufficient nitrogen, the chlorophyll production is impaired, leading to the yellowing of the leaves as the chlorophyll breaks down.

