0 -> [Which types of pathogens have the ability to cross a graft in commonly grafted rootstock and scion fruit trees?]
Based on the image, it's not possible to definitively identify the specific pathogens present, but generally, pathogens that can cross graft lines in fruit trees include bacteria, fungi, and viruses. For example, bacteria like Xanthomonas and Pseudomonas can cause diseases such as fire blight in apple trees, while fungi like Armillaria can cause root rot in a variety of tree species. Viruses can also be transmitted through grafting, affecting the health of the scion. The presence of any of these pathogens could lead to graft failure or disease in the grafted tree.

