0 -> [With which group are leaf galls like the ones on grapevines, caused by the aphid-like insect called Grape phylloxera - which has root and leaf feeding stages in its lifecycle and is considered an extremely atypical symptom for this insect group - more commonly associated?]
Leaf galls caused by the aphid-like insect Grape phylloxera are more commonly associated with the group of leaf galls that are caused by the family Cynipidae. This family includes a wide variety of gall-forming insects that produce galls on a range of plant species, including grapevines. The galls can vary in appearance and are often associated with specific plant families or genera. Grape phylloxera, which is known for its role in the phylloxera epidemic that devastated grapevines in the late 19th century, is indeed an atypical symptom for this group of gall-forming insects, as most are not associated with root feeding. However, the galls it produces are still considered a form of gall and are part of the broader category of plant galls caused by the Cynipidae family.

