0 -> [What are these dense formations observed in the canopies of birches, a common characteristic noticeable particularly in Abinger Hammer, Surrey?]
The dense formations observed in the canopies of birches are known as birch gall. These are large, irregularly shaped growths that can be found on the branches of birch trees. They are caused by the activity of certain insects, such as the birch gall wasp, which lays its eggs on the bark of birch trees. When the eggs hatch, the larvae feed on the sap and tissues of the tree, causing the growth of the gall. The gall can be a significant problem for birch trees, as it can weaken the tree and make it more susceptible to disease and insect attack.

