0 -> [Which group of pests or pathogens are these small emerging leaves from this bramble, a common sign of regrowth after applying glyphosate, most likely indicative of?]
The small emerging leaves from the bramble are most likely indicative of regrowth from the surviving roots of the bramble plant. Glyphosate is a herbicide that inhibits the shikimate pathway, which is essential for the synthesis of amino acids in plants. When applied to plants, it can cause the death of above-ground parts, but the roots can often survive and regrow from them. The regrowth seen here is a natural response of the plant to the removal of its above-ground parts, and it is not typically associated with pests or pathogens but rather with the plant's own recovery mechanisms.

